    "matrices": "Y in R^(n×N), D in R^(n×K) with unit-norm columns, X in R^(K×N); finite samples and a chosen sparsity budget T0. The source targets overcomplete dictionaries, while the block algebra also admits square constructed examples.",
    "objective": "Minimize ||Y−DX||_F² subject to at most T0 nonzero coefficients in each sample column.",
    "coding": "The paper permits a chosen sparse approximation procedure; changing the coder or acceptance safeguard changes operational guarantees. No general exact sparse-coding solution is assumed."
  },
  "evidence_linked_claims": [
    {
      "topic": "Identity and objective",
      "assessment": "Support one algorithm identity: K-SVD learns a synthesis dictionary and sparse sample coefficients by alternating coding with sequential singular-vector atom updates. Intended overcomplete use is part of the paper scope; a generic sparse-learning class is broader.",
      "locators": "Section IV opening, PDF pp.5–6 / printed pp.4315–4316, Eqs.17–19; Figure 2 p.7."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Support-restricted block",
      "assessment": "For atom k form E_k=Y−sum_{j≠k} d_j x_j^row; choose active samples Ω_k={i in 1..N:x_{ki}≠0}; restrict E_k to those columns. A full-residual SVD without restriction may introduce new coefficients and exceed the sample sparsity budget.",
      "locators": "PDF p.7 / printed p.4317, Eqs.20–23 and Figure 2; source caveat KSVD-S2."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Joint atom and coefficient update",
      "assessment": "A leading singular triplet of the restricted residual supplies a unit-norm atom and the associated scaled right singular vector as its active coefficient row. Scatter inactive entries as zero and use the newest updated coefficients for subsequent atoms.",
      "locators": "PDF p.7 / printed p.4317, Figure 2 and text following Eq.23."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Descent limit",
      "assessment": "An exact restricted rank-one update minimizes that block and cannot increase its loss. This does not imply arbitrary sparse recoding decreases the total objective. The source suggests comparing the previous-support fit with the new pursuit fit and retaining the better option; even monotone objective values alone do not prove convergence to a local minimizer.",
      "locators": "PDF p.8 / printed p.4318, convergence discussion and Eq.24; exact negative control in ksvd-boundaries.json."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Degeneracies and identifiability",
      "assessment": "An empty active set does not determine an atom; replacing unpopular atoms is an additional heuristic. A zero residual permits zero coefficients and any unit atom. Repeated leading singular values can produce different equally optimal rank-one reconstructions, and sign/permutation ambiguities preclude a unique learned representation without further conditions.",
      "locators": "Figure 2 p.7 and Section IV.E / Remarks p.8; degeneracy conclusions are curator algebra, supplemented by explicit exact cases rather than attributed as a source theorem."
    },
    {
      "topic": "K-means and application boundaries",
      "assessment": "Free scalar coefficients and normalized atoms form a different block problem from fixed-one coefficients with unconstrained centroids. T0=1 alone does not identify ordinary k-means. The fixed DC atom used in the image application is a configuration, not a universal algorithm requirement.",
      "locators": "Section IV.A, PDF p.6; Section V image application pp.9–10; explicit normalization example in ksvd-boundaries.json."
    }
  ],
  "source_caveats": [
    {
      "id": "KSVD-S1",
      "locator": "PDF p.6 / printed p.4316, Eq.15",
      "observation": "The printed sum in the k-means objective runs over i=1..K even though the sample count is N.",
      "handling": "Use the established N-sample convention when explaining sample objectives; preserve this discrepancy explicitly rather than silently copying K."
    },
    {
      "id": "KSVD-S2",
      "locator": "PDF p.7 / printed p.4317, Eq.22 versus Figure 2",
      "observation": "The printed support set uses 1<=i<=K, although i indexes N sample columns. Figure 2 uses the sample range N.",
      "handling": "Define the active set over samples 1..N, attribute the convention to Figure 2, and retain the Eq.22 indexing caveat. The exact example N=3,K=2 demonstrates the omitted active sample."
    },
    {
      "id": "KSVD-S3",
      "locator": "PDF p.3 / printed p.4313, Eqs.6–8 in prior maximum-likelihood method context",
      "observation": "The displayed Gaussian residual and Laplace coefficient factors have positive exponents, whereas Eq.8 minimizes positive residual and sparsity penalties.",
      "handling": "This context discrepancy is not part of the Figure 2 K-SVD update. Do not reproduce those factors or claim that a repaired historical algorithm has been audited."
    }
